Sculpt and rig the **Sunfront Lancer Foundry**, a tall, slender spire whose
rail-arm slides up and down its shaft and whose focus-ring spins at the crown, as a
44x84x44 opaque-voxel model using only the `voxel-anim` tool. You paint discrete
opaque cells to build it and **author its two self-playing animations** as F-curves.

This test measures **creativity and craft**, not instruction-following: the brief
tells you *what the foundry is* and *how it must move*, but the parts you sculpt, how
you break the spire into moving pieces, where you place the joints, and how you shape
every form are **entirely yours to invent**. Work out the pieces a spire with a
sliding arm and a spinning ring needs.

## How sculpting and rigging works

The only way to place a voxel and edit the rig is the `voxel-anim` binary already
on your `PATH`. It is the sole channel: you build the model by calling it **one
operation at a time**, and the ordered operations you issue — recorded per part to
`parts/<part>.actions.json` — together with the rig you define are the
**authoritative output that is scored**, not the voxels on disk. Anything produced
another way (writing files directly, editing a preview by hand) is discarded and
flagged; only the recorded operations and the rig count.

Every voxel operation targets a **part** with `--part <name>`; each part is
sculpted **separately** in its own preview and log. The volume starts **empty**.
Voxels are **opaque** `#rrggbb` colors — there is no transparency. After each call
the binary re-renders that part's isometric preview to `parts/<part>.png` and
appends the operation to that part's log.

**You invent the rig.** Define whatever parts you need with `define-part` (giving each
a parent and a pivot), place joints on them with `define-joint`, and author the
required animations with `define-animation`/`add-keyframe`. Every voxel operation
targets a **part** with `--part <name>`, so define a part before you sculpt into it.
A blank workspace and a pre-seeded `rig.json` holding the two required animation
declarations are already in place at `{{workspace}}`.

Each operation is an ordinary subcommand with flags, naming its part, for example:

```
voxel-anim define-part --name shaft
voxel-anim fill-box --part shaft --x0 14 --y0 0 --z0 14 --x1 29 --y1 60 --z1 29 --color "#c69a4b"
```

(The part name above is just an example — name your own parts however you like.) Run
`voxel-anim --help` to list every operation **and** the rig subcommands (defining
parts, setting pivots, defining joints, and authoring animations with
`define-animation`/`add-keyframe`), and `voxel-anim <operation> --help` for one
operation's exact flags — that help text is the authoritative contract. **Read
`parts/<part>.png` after your calls** to see that part's progress, **and read the
assembled-scene previews under `scene/` (`scene/iso.png`, `scene/front.png`,
`scene/side.png`, `scene/top.png`)** — the whole spire composed from all your parts,
re-rendered after every operation. The scene views are how you confirm the parts
actually fit together: that the sliding arm sets into the mid-shaft and the ring
seats on the crown. Because each part is sculpted in the shared volume's coordinates,
position each part where it belongs on the finished spire; the scene simply composes
them there.

